\pard\b\fs20 The Secular King\b0\par
\fs16\par
In the year 132 a secular king is installed, who is to rule the land neutrally and end the supremacy of the churches. The first of this new royal line is Kamteius the First.\par
But as early as 143, Kamteius is ambushed and murdered by a group of paladins on the way to his summer residence. His son, Kamteius the Second, takes power.\par
Neither church, however, wants to be to blame for the attack. Each accuses the other side of being treacherous traitors who wanted to undermine the royal system. And so, in 144, the war of faith begins anew.\par
In these years the so-called Rebellion appears more and more. This group of the most varied Meduvians is fed up with the rule of the churches and tries to fight them violently with treacherous raids and assassinations.\par
In the year 160 the Rebellion even attempts a double attack on the center of church power: Telotiosus.\par
Despite their pincer attack and the aggressive siege, the paladins manage to hold out long enough, and in the end they get reinforcements from the other paladins spread across the land. So the paladins were able to beat back the rebels, and once again had time to bash each other's heads in.\par
To put an end to the war, the Council of Cra-Kla-Zoth decides in the year 180 to make four extremely powerful relics, each holding the power of one of the four elements. These were hidden in a remote valley.\par
The paladins learn of these ancient, mighty relics, which together would have enough power to wipe out the other side for all time. So they all set out to fight for the relics in this valley.\par
But since gathering the relics turned out to be extremely difficult, the war finally petered out in 185, and peace returned to the land.\par
With that the age of the wars of faith ended.\par
But when paladins came across Kanderians in the southern desert in 204, the peace was over. The paladins had soon started a war with these godless desert warriors. Against these strong fighters the paladins found it very hard to win. Since the paladins had their hands full with the Kanderians, the rebels now wanted to use this critical moment to stab the paladins in the back and finally overthrow the rule of the churches.\par
